WHAT A SURVEY OF THESE REPOSITORIES REPORTED. These are LEADS, not settled facts.

A single pass swept the whole estate with limited tools, and it has been wrong: on
2026-08-08 it reported that a repository contained no reference to a package that its
own source uses in twenty files, and a roster was minted on that. Treat every line
below as what the survey BELIEVES, to be confirmed by the investigator that owns the
codeline. Do NOT restate any of it in a brief as established, verified, or confirmed —
a brief is inherited whole and re-checked by nothing, so a wrong lead written as a fact
becomes an instruction.

Propose roles for the codelines the work appears to REACH. A codeline reported
no_work_found is not confirmed clear, and one reported not_investigated or failed was
not established either way — do not treat any of the three as proof about that repo:

- mocka: in_scope — areas: src/, test/
    files it opened: src/fares.ts, test/fares.test.ts
    evidence: Opened src/fares.ts and test/fares.test.ts. fareFor() has `if (rider.age > 65) return CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS;` — a rider aged exactly 65 falls through to the base-fare return of 350, matching MOCK3-1's description exactly. No test currently exercises age===65. Also confirmed via directory listing that mocka contains no schedule/stop-related file, so MOCK3-2 does not reach this codeline.
- mockb: in_scope — areas: src/, test/
    files it opened: src/schedule.ts, test/schedule.test.ts
    evidence: Opened src/schedule.ts and test/schedule.test.ts. formatStops() loops `for (let i = 0; i < stops.length - 1; i++)`, which excludes the final stop from the rendered parts array — matching MOCK3-2's description that a three-stop line renders only two. totalMinutes() correctly reads stops[stops.length-1], explaining why the omission wasn't caught by journey-length checks. No test currently asserts the last stop's name appears in formatStops output. Also confirmed via directory listing that mockb contains no fare-related file, so MOCK3-1 does not reach this codeline.

SEPARATELY — and this is a recommendation about the TEAM, not something discovered
about the code: a survey of the estate suggests these codelines need their own
investigator. Propose one per codeline named here, with the stated focus:

- mocka: focus on Confirm the exact boundary condition intended for the concession-fare age rule and how the fix interacts with the existing under-13 and pass-holder branches. (because fareFor() uses a strict `>` comparison against 65, and I confirmed via test/fares.test.ts that no existing test pins down the age===65 boundary.)
- mockb: focus on Confirm how formatStops' loop bound relates to totalMinutes' indexing and whether any other consumer of the stops array assumes the current (truncated) output. (because formatStops() stops one index short of the array's end, and test/schedule.test.ts only checks for 'Union' (the first stop) and the '->' separator, not the last stop's presence.)

AND — also a recommendation about the TEAM — the survey reports these codelines need
work WRITTEN, not merely read. An investigator cannot change code; every story is
assigned to an implementer, so propose at least one implementer that can build this:

- mocka: must be able to write Correct the age comparison in the fare rule and add a test pinning the exact-65 boundary to the concession fare. (because Read src/fares.ts and confirmed the off-by-boundary comparison directly causes MOCK3-1; a writer is needed to change the comparison and extend test/fares.test.ts.)
- mockb: must be able to write Correct the stop-rendering loop so the final stop is included, and add a test asserting the last stop's name appears in the formatted output. (because Read src/schedule.ts and confirmed the loop bound directly causes MOCK3-2; a writer is needed to fix the loop and extend test/schedule.test.ts.)
WHAT YOU MAY STATE AS FACT.

What a repository contains or declares — you or a survey read it, and the next agent can
read it too. State it freely.

What an external API, SDK or service accepts — its option names, field names, endpoints,
hosts, token kinds — you may NOT state as your own knowledge. If a document quoted above
says it, attribute it ("the linked documentation states..."). Otherwise instruct the role
to verify it against the installed package before relying on it.

Never write that one API key, field or option is correct and another is wrong. A claim of
that shape is inherited as an instruction, is not re-checked by anything, and has already
caused correct work to be rejected across three codelines.
WHO WRITES THE TESTS — NOT THESE ROLES.

A dedicated agent of this pipeline writes the tests. It takes its own turn after the fix
is committed and owns the reproducing test, and the roles you propose are separately
FORBIDDEN from creating or editing any test file.

So: do not propose a test-writing, QA or test-automation role — that work is already owned.
And a brief must not say the role writes, owns, colocates or maintains tests, in any words.
A role whose brief claims test authorship is handed two contradictory instructions and
spends its turns fighting itself. Describe what the role BUILDS.
You are a senior engineering manager designing an AI agent team for a software project.

The following 21 fixed roles are ALWAYS present and must NOT be reproduced:
spec-coordinator-agent, openspec-agent, speckit-agent, team-lead-agent, review-agent, test-coordinator-agent, sast-sentinel, spec-validator, review-ranger, mutant-hunter, fuzz-weaver, perf-sentinel, hygiene-sentinel, design-sentinel, pattern-sentinel, project-initiator-agent, prd-project-manager-agent, agent-skills-agent, dashboard-orchestrator-agent, dashboard-test-agent, dashboard-update-agent

Given the project manifest, tech stack, and clarification answers, propose 2-6 project-specific engineering agent roles that will implement the user stories. Each role should map to a distinct domain of the project — one per domain, e.g. a react-frontend role, a postgres-data role, an auth-security role. The exact name shape is fixed below.

Every role is one of TWO KINDS, and you must state which:

- "implementer" — WRITES the change. This is the role that edits source and makes the work
  happen. An implementer spans the project, so its codeline is "*".
- "investigator" — READS one codeline and characterises what is there for the implementer. It
  never writes. An investigator names exactly ONE codeline, spelled as listed in scope.

YOU MUST PROPOSE AT LEAST ONE IMPLEMENTER. A team of investigators can describe the work and
cannot do it: every story is assigned to an implementer, so a roster without one leaves every
story with nobody to write it and the run cannot proceed. Investigators are optional and
additive — propose them where a codeline genuinely needs its own reader.

The survey above recommends both: which codelines need investigating, and which need work
written. Those are recommendations about the team, not instructions — you decide the roster.

For each role provide:
- name: kebab-case, "<domain>-<suffix>", where the suffix is fixed by the kind:
  - implementer: ends in "-engineer" or "-fixer"
  - investigator: ends in "-detective" or "-investigator"
  No other ending is routable — a name outside these shapes cannot be assigned to any seam
  and the run stops at the mint.
- kind: exactly "implementer" or "investigator"
- codeline: for an investigator, the ONE codeline it reads; for an implementer, "*"
- systemPrompt: A detailed system prompt (150-300 words) describing the agent's expertise, the project's conventions, key files/directories they own, coding patterns they follow, and tools they use. Be specific to this project.
- rationale: One sentence explaining why THIS project needs THIS role. It is read by a human
  reviewing the team and seeds the agent's knowledge base, so it must say something.

Respond with ONLY valid JSON (no markdown fences):
{
  "proposedAgents": [
    { "name": "<string>", "kind": "<implementer|investigator>", "codeline": "<name or *>", "systemPrompt": "<string>", "rationale": "<string>" }
  ]
}

THE WORK THIS PROJECT HAS BEEN ASKED TO DO (real tickets from the tracker):
- MOCK3-1: Riders aged exactly 65 are charged the adult fare
  The fare rules say riders aged 65 and over pay the concession fare. A rider aged exactly 65 is charged the full adult fare of 350 cents instead of 175. Riders aged 66 and above are charged correctly, so the boundary itself is wrong rather than the rule.
- MOCK3-2: The departure board omits the final stop
  formatStops renders every stop except the last one, so a three-stop line shows two. The journey length reported by totalMinutes is correct, which is why the omission has gone unnoticed.

